IMPORT prelude

-- Contributory Negligence and Personal Injuries Act 1953 (Singapore) -- the whole operative Act: the definitions of "fault",
-- "damage" and "dependant" (s 2), apportionment of damages where the injured person was partly at fault, with its savings for
-- contractual defences, liability caps, the recorded total, dependants' claims and time-barred parties (s 3(1)-(6)), the
-- exclusion of Maritime Conventions Act claims (s 4), and the abolition of the defence of common employment (s 5).
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition, as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/CNPIA1953.txt. The cover says it "incorporates all
-- amendments up to and including 1 December 2021 and comes into operation on 31 December 2021". No later amendment is annotated
-- in the text; the last entry in the deposited legislative history is the 2002 Revised Edition, and the last amending Act it lists
-- is Act 45 of 1998 (Civil Law (Amendment) Act 1998, commencement 1 January 1999). SSO's metadata calls it the "Current version as
-- at 01 Oct 2026".
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Chosen for its everyday-life relevance: this is the Act that decides what happens to a damages claim when the
-- injured person was partly to blame -- the pedestrian who stepped out without looking, the passenger without a seat belt, the
-- worker who ignored a safety rule. SCOPE: the Act has five sections and all of the operative ones are encoded. Not encoded: how
-- a court fixes the share of responsibility (the Act leaves it to what is "just and equitable"); the content of sections 10, 20
-- and 21 of the Civil Law Act 1909, section 1 of the Maritime Conventions Act 1911 and the Limitation Act 1959, which this Act
-- refers to but which were not read; contribution between wrongdoers; and any case law.
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text, following the conventions of the example naive rows. No pipeline, no coverage
-- table, no independent test pass, no human gate. See NOTES.md.

§ `Contributory Negligence and Personal Injuries Act 1953 -- apportionment`

GIVEN `a` IS A NUMBER, `b` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the lesser of` MEANS IF `a` AT MOST `b` THEN `a` ELSE `b`

§§ `2 -- fault, damage and personal injury`

-- s 2: "fault" means "negligence, breach of statutory duty or other act or omission which gives rise to a liability in tort or
-- would, apart from this Act, give rise to the defence of contributory negligence". "damage" "includes loss of life and personal
-- injury". "court" includes an arbitrator.
--
-- The second limb catches conduct that wrongs nobody else -- a person's carelessness for their own safety -- so the claimant's
-- own lapse counts as "fault" without being a tort. A breach of contract that is not also a tort is in neither limb on the words
-- of the definition (inference: the definition does not mention contract at all).
DECLARE Conduct IS ONE OF
    `negligence towards another`
    `a breach of statutory duty`
    `another act or omission giving rise to liability in tort`
    `carelessness for one's own safety that would give rise to the defence of contributory negligence`
    `a breach of contract that is not a tort`
    `blameless conduct`

GIVEN `the conduct` IS A Conduct
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the conduct is fault` IF
    CONSIDER `the conduct`
    WHEN `a breach of contract that is not a tort` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `blameless conduct` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

-- s 5(3): "'personal injury' includes any disease and any impairment of a person's physical or mental condition". Property
-- damage and pure financial loss are not personal injury on any ordinary reading (inference: the definition is inclusive and does
-- not exclude anything expressly).
DECLARE Harm IS ONE OF
    `a broken leg`
    `an occupational disease`
    `a psychiatric impairment`
    `damage to the claimant's car`
    `lost profits alone`

GIVEN `the harm` IS A Harm
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the harm is personal injury` IF
    CONSIDER `the harm`
    WHEN `damage to the claimant's car` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `lost profits alone` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

§§ `3(1), 4 -- when apportionment applies`

-- s 3(1): "Where any person suffers damage as the result partly of his own fault and partly of the fault of any other person or
-- persons, a claim in respect of that damage shall NOT BE DEFEATED by reason of the fault of the person suffering the damage, but
-- the damages recoverable ... shall be REDUCED to such extent as the court thinks just and equitable having regard to the
-- claimant's share in the responsibility for the damage."
-- s 4: "This Act shall not apply to any claims to which section 1 of the Maritime Conventions Act 1911 applies and that Act shall
-- have effect as if this Act had not been passed."
-- s 3(2): "Subsection (1) shall not operate to defeat any defence arising under a contract."
GIVEN `own fault contributed` IS A BOOLEAN, `another's fault contributed` IS A BOOLEAN, `a Maritime Conventions Act claim` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 3 applies` IF
    `own fault contributed` AND `another's fault contributed` AND (NOT `a Maritime Conventions Act claim`)

GIVEN `own fault contributed` IS A BOOLEAN, `another's fault contributed` IS A BOOLEAN, `a Maritime Conventions Act claim` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `a contractual defence covers the claim` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 3 saves the claim from the claimant's own fault` IF
    `section 3 applies` `own fault contributed` `another's fault contributed` `a Maritime Conventions Act claim`
    AND (NOT `a contractual defence covers the claim`)

-- The claim "shall not be defeated", yet the reduction is to "such extent as the court thinks just and equitable". A 100 per cent
-- reduction would leave nothing, which is the claim defeated by another name. Inference, not in the text: the reduction must be
-- less than 100 per cent.
GIVEN `reduction percent` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the reduction is within section 3(1)` IF
    `reduction percent` AT LEAST 0 AND `reduction percent` LESS THAN 100

§§ `3(1), 3(3) -- the damages recoverable`

-- s 3(3): "Where any contract or written law providing for the limitation of liability is applicable to the claim, the amount of
-- damages recoverable by the claimant UNDER SUBSECTION (1) shall not exceed the maximum limit so applicable."
--
-- The cap bites on the amount recoverable under s 3(1), which is the REDUCED figure. So the reduction is made from the full damages
-- first and the cap applied afterwards (reading of the words "recoverable ... under subsection (1)"); a claimant whose full damages
-- far exceed the cap may lose nothing by being partly at fault.
GIVEN `full damages` IS A NUMBER, `reduction percent` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the reduced damages` MEANS
    `full damages` TIMES (100 MINUS `reduction percent`) DIVIDED BY 100

GIVEN `full damages` IS A NUMBER, `reduction percent` IS A NUMBER, `a liability limit applies` IS A BOOLEAN, `the limit` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the damages recoverable` MEANS
    IF `a liability limit applies`
    THEN `the lesser of` (`the reduced damages` `full damages` `reduction percent`) `the limit`
    ELSE `the reduced damages` `full damages` `reduction percent`

-- s 3(4): "the court SHALL find and record the total damages which would have been recoverable if the claimant had not been at
-- fault."
GIVEN `own fault contributed` IS A BOOLEAN, `another's fault contributed` IS A BOOLEAN, `a Maritime Conventions Act claim` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the court must record the total damages` IF
    `section 3 applies` `own fault contributed` `another's fault contributed` `a Maritime Conventions Act claim`

§§ `3(5) -- deaths and dependants`

-- s 3(5): where a person dies "as the result partly of his own fault and partly of the fault of any other person", and an estate
-- action under s 10 of the Civil Law Act 1909 would be reduced under (1), damages in an action for the benefit of (a) "the
-- dependants of that person under section 20" or (b) "any person under section 21" of that Act "shall be reduced to a
-- proportionate extent". "dependant" (s 2): any person for whose benefit an action could be brought under s 20.
--
-- "Proportionate extent" is read here as the same percentage as the estate's reduction (inference).
GIVEN `the deceased's fault contributed` IS A BOOLEAN, `another's fault contributed to the death` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `dependants' full damages` IS A NUMBER, `estate reduction percent` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the dependants' damages` MEANS
    IF `the deceased's fault contributed` AND `another's fault contributed to the death`
    THEN `the reduced damages` `dependants' full damages` `estate reduction percent`
    ELSE `dependants' full damages`

§§ `3(6) -- the party who pleads a time bar`

-- s 3(6): where one of the persons at fault "avoids liability to any other such person or his personal representative by pleading
-- the Limitation Act 1959 or any other written law limiting the time within which proceedings may be taken, he shall NOT be
-- entitled to recover any damages or contributions from that other person ... by virtue of that subsection."
GIVEN `both were at fault for the damage` IS A BOOLEAN, `pleaded a time bar against the other's claim` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 3 bars recovery by the party who pleaded the time bar` IF
    `both were at fault for the damage` AND `pleaded a time bar against the other's claim`

§§ `5 -- common employment`

-- s 5(1): "It shall NOT be a defence to an employer who is sued in respect of personal injuries caused by the negligence of a person
-- employed by him, that that person was at the time the injuries were caused in common employment with the person injured."
GIVEN `the employer is sued for personal injuries` IS A BOOLEAN, `caused by negligence of someone the employer employed` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the negligent employee was in common employment with the injured person` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 5 removes the defence of common employment` IF
    `the employer is sued for personal injuries` AND `caused by negligence of someone the employer employed`
    AND `the negligent employee was in common employment with the injured person`

-- s 5(2): "Any provision in a contract of service or apprenticeship, or in an agreement collateral thereto shall be VOID insofar as it
-- would have the effect of excluding or limiting any liability of the employer in respect of personal injuries caused to the person
-- employed or apprenticed by the negligence of persons in common employment with him."
-- A contract for services with an independent contractor is not a "contract of service" (inference from the ordinary legal
-- distinction; the Act does not define either term).
DECLARE Instrument IS ONE OF
    `a contract of service`
    `a contract of apprenticeship`
    `an agreement collateral to a contract of service`
    `a contract for services with an independent contractor`

GIVEN `the instrument` IS A Instrument
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the instrument is within section 5(2)` IF
    CONSIDER `the instrument`
    WHEN `a contract for services with an independent contractor` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

GIVEN `the instrument` IS A Instrument, `excludes or limits the employer's liability` IS A BOOLEAN, `the harm` IS A Harm,
      `caused by negligence of a person in common employment` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the provision is void under section 5(2)` IF
    `the instrument is within section 5(2)` `the instrument`
    AND `excludes or limits the employer's liability`
    AND `the harm is personal injury` `the harm`
    AND `caused by negligence of a person in common employment`
